  "text": "I can't verify these against the literature, so this is a best-effort judgment, not a confirmed check.\n\nSolutions 1 and 3 agree that the degree-4 case is proved (Braun and Oréfice-Okamoto), and that degree-3 is also covered. They disagree on degree 5. Solution 1 says there are non-injective examples with a degree-5 component. Solution 3 says degree 5 forces injectivity and that a degree-6 counterexample exists. Both can't be right.\n\nI have a faint recollection of a published result that a component of degree at most 5 forces injectivity, which would make Solution 1's degree-5 counterexample wrong. I also don't recall any non-injective example with a component of degree 6 or 5. The known Pinchuk-type examples have much higher degrees, so Solution 3's sharpness claim is also unverified.\n\nSolution 2 is unsupported. It bounds the total degree of the map, which is not what the problem asks, and its claimed threshold of 6 has no real basis.\n\nI lean toward Solution 3 (answer 5), but I'm not confident in its sharpness claim.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
